# ============================================================
# novi-recon — network reconnaissance, with nothing but the standard
# library
#
# RFC 0028. This exists because RFC 0026/0027 put a Python with working
# TLS on the machine and nothing yet used it, and because the obvious
# thing to reach for could not be shipped: the "God's Eye" information
# gathering tool on GitHub is `Copyright ... All rights reserved` with
# no redistribution grant, so it cannot go in a signed repository this
# project hands to other people. The capability is worth having; the
# code is not ours to give away. So this covers the same ground from
# scratch.
#
# THE HARD RULE HERE IS: STANDARD LIBRARY ONLY.
#
# Not a preference. The tool it replaces needs requests, dnspython,
# python-nmap, folium, opencage and phonenumbers -- six PyPI packages,
# two of which want an API key and one of which pulls a numeric stack
# in behind it -- plus the nmap and httpie binaries. This system has no
# pip (RFC 0026) and packaging six third-party trees to run one script
# is not a trade anybody would make. Everything below is written
# against `socket`, `struct`, `ssl`, `urllib`, `hashlib` and
# `concurrent.futures`, all of which are already in the `python`
# package.
#
# It also means the DNS and WHOIS clients are written out longhand.
# That is a feature: `dns_lookup` in the tool this replaces is a
# dnspython call, and dnspython is 25k lines to ask one question.
#
# AUTHORISED USE. `ports` is a TCP connect scan and `pwned` sends a
# hash prefix to a third party. Both are ordinary, both are things a
# person should only point at systems they are allowed to test, and the
# --help says so rather than leaving it implied.
# ============================================================

